#{[17592186233093 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5-1}-2}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5} & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x-1}-2)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x-1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x-1-4}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x-5}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5-1}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233097 :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+2)(2x-1)}{(x-2)(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne \\pm 2,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=-\\frac{2x-1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=2.$ So statement II is false.\n\nTo find  $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ | $2$ | $2.001$ | $2.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $298$ | $2\\,998$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-3\\,002$ | $-302$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 2$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 2$ from the right . Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e96d6a81813c1c89a498d0206234f2685f0a53c1bab626ce7430d84f9c0f506.png)"] [17592186233193 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4i}{n}-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233196 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{2k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233198 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233211 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]}^{'}}{[8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2x-6}{\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[x-3]}^{'}}{[2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{-(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to 3}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-(3+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233221 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}x=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{ln}^{2}x]^{'}}{{[x]}^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}\\right)}{1} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}x)^{2}}{x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}x]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233222 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)=-\\infty \n$$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x} & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)]^{'}}{{[\\operatorname{tan}x]}^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\right)}{{sec}^{2}x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x{cos}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233223 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}2x=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{cot}2x]^{'}}{[\\operatorname{cot}x]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-2{csc}^{2}2x)}{-{csc}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}2x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{{sin}^{2}2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{{(2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{4{sin}^{2}x{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2\\cancel{{sin}^{2}x}}{4\\cancel{{sin}^{2}x}{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233332 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})=-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})=-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})=-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=-\\sqrt{3}n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(-\\sqrt{3}n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{3})\n$$ is also divergent."] [17592186233334 :question/worked-solution "The given series is an arithmetic series. Its first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand its $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{n}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we get the following formula for the $n$th partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{n} & =\\frac{n}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{n}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n(1+n)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the limit of the partial sums, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n(1+n)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{n}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the limit of the sequence of partial sums is infinite, the series is divergent."] [17592186233335 :question/worked-solution "The given series is an arithmetic series. Its first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1+3=4,\n$$\n\nIts $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=n+3.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we get the following formula for the $n$th partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{n} & =\\frac{n}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{2}(4+n+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{n(7+n)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the limit of the partial sums, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n(7+n)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{n}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the limit of the sequence of partial sums is infinite, the series is divergent."] [17592186233378 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u={x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{{x}^{2}}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n/x} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={({e}^{{x}^{2}})}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233390 :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=2\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn part 5, we found that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2,$ and from part 3, we found $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}) & =2\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 0+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-0+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233391 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot 2 & =6 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233414 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233418 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\n![](images/42/42869c56604cbec85b5e4915f77b42e72444b7d9d117e140d9eba56b58c0f131.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $3\\pi $ from the left, the values of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\n$$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233419 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{2-2}{(2)^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233420 :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}[\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233421 :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\sqrt{3}f(x))}{\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n* applying the constant multiple rule in the numerator, and \n\n* the root rule, followed by the sum rule in the denominator, \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\sqrt{3}f(x))}{\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3)^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)}{{(\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (3\\sqrt{3})}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233443 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion."] [17592186233491 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi /6}{v}_{2}(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to \\pi /6}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(3t) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233492 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4{csc}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =4{csc}^{2}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =4\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}t.$\n\n![](images/36/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}t$ are positive and grow without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}t=\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4{csc}^{2}(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}{csc}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\lim_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $4$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n4\\cdot {(\\lim_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233507 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}(x)) & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233509 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{x-3}{3}\\right):$ \n\n![](images/ee/ee481869c72bd5858ac5f2c561605b3b164555a4043eb914d49da7506dcf40dd.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{x-3}{3}\\right)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{x-3}{3}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233510 :question/worked-solution "![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233511 :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-x-3, & x<-3 \\\\ 0, & x=-3 \\\\ x+3, & x>-3\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-3$\nII. - $\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)$\nIII. - $\\lim_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=f(-3)$\nIV. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3$"] [17592186233511 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that\n\n$$\nf(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-3.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-x-3) \\\\  & =-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(x+3) \\\\  & =(-3)+3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-3$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=0=f(-3).$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [17592186233512 :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}4x+12, & x\\ne -2 \\\\ 4, & x=-2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$\nII. - $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$\nIII. - $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)$\nIV. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$"] [17592186233512 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(4x+12) \\\\  & =4(-2)+12 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(4x+12) \\\\  & =4(-2)+12 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=4\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=4=f(-2).$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [17592186233545 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term of the polynomial and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(2x-{x}^{2}) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233546 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit directly by substituting $x=2,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}}{2(2)-(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since there is a radical in the numerator, we can evaluate the limit by first multiplying the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})}{(2x-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{x-2}{x(2-x)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{-(2-x)}{x(2-x)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{2-x}}{x\\cancel{(2-x)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233547 :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3) & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{[2x-{x}^{2}]-[2(3)-(3)^{2}]}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x+3}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{-({x}^{2}-2x-3)}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{-(x+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{x-3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}(-x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233548 :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(-x-1) & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}x-\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}1 \\\\  & =-(3)-1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233549 :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-(x+h)^{2}-(2x-{x}^{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-{x}^{2}-2xh-{h}^{2}-2x+{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h-2hx-{h}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(2-2x-h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(2-2x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233550 :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}(2-2x-h) & =\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}(2-2x)-\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}h \\\\  & =2-2x-0 \\\\  & =2-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233586 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+2}+1.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =\\sqrt{-2+2}+1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}+1. \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/d0/d0e6532c8b96d7a3ab85d73a145a79292b8b0101cfa46ebffb08aefdeb06687b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we notice that for the left-hand limit, we have:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand limit, we have:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=1\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186233588 :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}{\\left(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{\\lim_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=4.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)=4(4)-12=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)={3}^{2}-2(3)+2=5\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}{\\left(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{\\lim_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233758 :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-8$ causes $y=-8{x}^{2}$ to become a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-8{x}^{2})=-\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233759 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}-6{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-15) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{8}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233760 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -1}(2x+8)}{\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and the denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}(2x+8) & =2\\lim_{x\\to -1}x+\\lim_{x\\to -1}8 \\\\  & =2(-1)+8 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\lim_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{4}+5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3(-1)^{4}+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}} & =\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233761 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the powers and multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{4}(x)\\cdot {q}^{3}(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({(p(x))}^{4}\\cdot {(q(x))}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({(-2x)}^{4}\\cdot {(-2x)}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(16{x}^{4}\\cdot -8{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }((16\\cdot -8){x}^{4+3}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-128\\cdot {x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-128\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{7}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-128$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-128\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7} & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{7} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-128{x}^{7})=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233762 :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{6}(4x-2)$ is well defined for $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 2}{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{\\lim_{x\\to 2}(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}(4\\cdot 2-2)}{2+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}({6}^{1})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233764 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233765 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233766 :question/worked-solution "Since we know that the limit of $\\sqrt{5x-m}$ exists, we can substitute $x=2$ into this expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m} & =\\sqrt{5(2)-m} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $\\sqrt{5},$ we can equate the above with $\\sqrt{5},$ and then solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10-m} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10-m & =5 \\\\ m & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233779 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=-1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4} & =\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{[x+1]^{'}}{[3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(-1)^{2}-2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233780 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(x+4)\\sqrt{x+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(0+4)\\sqrt{0+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{4(2)}]}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233858 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [17592186233860 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}+5x-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}+5x-6$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [17592186233868 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{-5{x}^{9}}{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-5}{2}\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is a power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $\\frac{-5}{2}$ changes the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9} & =-\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=\\infty . & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{-5{x}^{9}}{2}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233869 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & =\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{′}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{′}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9\\lim_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233870 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1]}^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5){5}^{x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5){e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233871 :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot f(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (3{x}^{9})=3{x}^{11}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)$ and $g(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\cdot g(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{11}\\cdot (-2{x}^{-11})) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }-6 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233874 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{8(x-1)}+2) & =\\sqrt[3]{8(1-1)}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{0}+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233877 :question/worked-solution "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}} & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+2))}^{4}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to 0}(f(x)-5))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+2)=2+2=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(f(x)-5)=2-5=-3\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}} & =\\frac{{4}^{4}}{(-3)^{3}}=\\frac{256}{-27}=-\\frac{256}{27}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233878 :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=4.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3}=\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x))^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{2}+3)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(4+3)}^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2} \\\\  & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x))^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & ={(5-4)}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3} & =49\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233879 :question/worked-solution "Applying the root rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 25}{x}^{-1/2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to 25}x)}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =(25)^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={({5}^{2})}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={5}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233880 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}g(x)=3.$\n\nApplying the root and the quotient rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)}{\\,2f(x)+g(x)\\,}} & =\\sqrt{\\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)}{\\,2f(x)+g(x)\\,}\\,} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\,\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 0}((x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x))}{\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+g(x))}\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}((x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)) & =(0+3)^{3}-(2)(3) \\\\  & =27-6 \\\\  & =21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2f(x)+g(x)) & =2(2)+3 \\\\  & =4+3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)}{\\,2f(x)+g(x)\\,}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{7}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233881 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}(\\operatorname{csc}(3x)+6x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =1+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233882 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233883 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(2)}-2}{2-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x}-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x-4}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(2)}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233884 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3(0)+25}}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{3x+25})(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x+25})}^{2}}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{25-(3x+25)}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3x}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3x+25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3(0)+25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233885 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(2)-2}-\\sqrt{2+2}}{2-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2})(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{3x-2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3x-2)-(x+2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x-4}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3(2)-2}+\\sqrt{2+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233886 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-9)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x+1)-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{x-3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{\\cancel{x-3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\,(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2) \\\\  & =(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2) \\\\  & =6(4) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233887 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3(x+2)}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233889 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi } & =\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)}{{1}^{2}+\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\cdot 0}{1+\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233892 :question/worked-solution "![](images/68/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(x)) & =sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)) \\\\  & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233893 :question/worked-solution "Since $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x)=g(3)=16.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(4g(x)-3x\\sqrt{g(3)}) & =4(\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x))-(\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}3x)\\sqrt{g(3)} \\\\  & =4(16)-3(3)\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =64-9(4) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233894 :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ 5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x) & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n$\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186233894 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\pi -\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge \\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}(5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & =5+2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5+2(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$"] [17592186233895 :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)$ is well defined for $x>-7$ and $x<5,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x) & ={log}_{3}(2+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-2) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(9)\\cdot {log}_{3}(3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}({3}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233898 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-2)^{2}+3x-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186233898 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=1$ from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+3x-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}[(x-2)^{2}+3x-5] \\\\  & =(1-2)^{2}+3(1)-5 \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+3-5 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=4$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}(4))+2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233907 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}c-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge -1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233907 :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ to be equal to $8,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c-x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}c-x \\\\  & =c-(-1) \\\\  & =c+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{x}^{2}-2x-1 \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-2(-1)-1 \\\\  & =1+2-1 \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ c+1 & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233923 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(0)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=5.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10-f(0)}{(f(0))^{2}} & =\\frac{10-5}{(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233929 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}{(4{x}^{2}-5f(x))}^{3} & ={(4(3)^{2}-5(7))}^{3} \\\\  & =(36-35)^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233930 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(4)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[(f(4))^{2}-11]}^{4} & ={[(-3)^{2}-11]}^{4} \\\\  & =(-2)^{4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233947 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233948 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cot}(x)) & =\\operatorname{sin}(0)+4\\operatorname{cot}(0) \\\\  & =0+4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(0)}{\\operatorname{sin}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec2bf84d52026890b6796c30f3cd772b7cb34230200acf40b6e19bce7d891343.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}x$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}x=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $4$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n \n\n$$\n4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}x=4\\cdot (-\\infty )=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cot}(x))=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233954 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}(x)) & =tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}(1)) \\\\  & =tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233960 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{1/2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{n}=\\frac{9}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{9}{n})\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{9i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(\\,1+\\frac{9i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,1/2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233961 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=7{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[7{(\\,1+\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233992 :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2} & =\\lim_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to 1}(4y-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{y\\to 1}(2{y}^{2}+1))}^{3}\\cdot {(\\lim_{y\\to 1}(4y-1))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(2{(1)}^{2}+1)}^{3}{(4(1)-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{3}(3)^{2} \\\\  & =243\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233993 :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{z\\to (-4)}\\frac{{({z}^{2}-10)}^{2}}{(z+6)^{5}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{z\\to (-4)}{({z}^{2}-10)}^{2}}{\\lim_{z\\to (-4)}(z+6)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{z\\to (-4)}({z}^{2}-10))}^{2}}{{(\\lim_{z\\to (-4)}(z+6))}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{((-4)^{2}-10)}^{2}}{(-4+6)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{2}}{{2}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233994 :question/worked-solution "Applying the root rule, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[5]{(7x+11)^{3}} & =\\sqrt[5]{\\lim_{x\\to -1}(7x+11)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{{(\\lim_{x\\to -1}(7x+11))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{(7(-1)+11)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{{2}^{5}\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[5]{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233995 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to 1/2}f(x)=1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x} & =\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to 1/2}(4(f(x))^{4}+10x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(1)^{4}+10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233997 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{-2}\\right)}^{3} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{3}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{3}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{8}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}\\right)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233998 :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}=\\lim_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x))^{4}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3(-1)^{2}-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x))^{4} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x)))}^{4} \\\\  & ={(2-1)}^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1))}^{2}{(\\lim_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x)))}^{4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233999 :question/worked-solution "Applying the root rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to 128}{y}^{-1/7} & ={(\\lim_{y\\to 128}y)}^{-1/7} \\\\  & =(128)^{-1/7} \\\\  & ={({2}^{7})}^{-1/7} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234000 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7{x}^{3}+\\sqrt{2}{x}^{2}+6x-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\,(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234002 :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=0$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-x+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}x+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186234002 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(-x+1) \\\\  & =-(0)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}x+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}0+1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$"] [17592186234003 :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=-2$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{2}^{x}-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2-x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-2<x<2.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186234003 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={2}^{x}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(-2)}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(-2)}^{-}}({2}^{x}-3) \\\\  & ={2}^{-2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{2-x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(-2)}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(-2)}^{+}}\\sqrt{2-x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186234004 :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=1$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-{x}^{2}-x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186234004 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-{x}^{2}-x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}-x+2) \\\\  & =-(1)^{2}-1+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-{x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186234005 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+cx+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$"] [17592186234005 :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+b) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2(1)+b \\\\  & =1+2+b \\\\  & =b+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ b+3 & =2 \\\\ b & =2-3 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+cx+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{2}+cx+2) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+c(1)+2 \\\\  & =1+c+2 \\\\  & =c+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ c+3 & =2 \\\\ c & =2-3 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
